.IX Title "Koha::File::Transport::FTP 3pm"
.TH Koha::File::Transport::FTP 3pm "2026-10-29" "perl v5.32.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH "NAME"
Koha::File::Transport::FTP \- FTP implementation of file transport
.SS "Class methods"
.IX Subsection "Class methods"
\fIconnect\fR
.IX Subsection "connect"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $success = $self\->connect;
.Ve
.PP
Start the \s-1FTP\s0 transport connect, returns true on success or undefined on failure.
.PP
\fIupload_file\fR
.IX Subsection "upload_file"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $success =  $transport\->upload_file($fh);
.Ve
.PP
Passed a filehandle, this will upload the file to the current directory of the server connection.
.PP
Returns true on success or undefined on failure.
.PP
\fIdownload_file\fR
.IX Subsection "download_file"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $success =  $transport\->download_file($filename);
.Ve
.PP
Passed a filename, this will download the file from the current directory of the server connection.
.PP
Returns true on success or undefined on failure.
.PP
\fIchange_directory\fR
.IX Subsection "change_directory"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $success = $server\->change_directory($directory);
.Ve
.PP
Passed a directory name, this will change the current directory of the server connection.
.PP
Returns true on success or undefined on failure.
.PP
\fIlist_files\fR
.IX Subsection "list_files"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $files = $server\->list_files;
.Ve
.PP
Returns an array reference of hashrefs with file information found in the current directory of the server connection.
Each hashref contains: filename, longname, size, perms, mtime, type.
.PP
The listing is obtained with \s-1MLSD\s0 (\s-1RFC 3659\s0), whose machine-readable output
is locale\- and platform-independent and gives us the filename unambiguously
(the pathname is the entire text after the \*(L"facts\*(R" and a single space, so
filenames containing spaces are handled correctly). Servers that do not
support \s-1MLSD\s0 fall back to \s-1NLST,\s0 which returns bare filenames only.
.PP
This mirrors what Net::FTP does internally for \fBrmdir()\fR. Net::FTP 3.x exposes
no public \s-1MLSD\s0 method, so we drive it through its \fB_list_cmd()\fR helper \- the
same private method that backs the public \fBls()\fR/\fBdir()\fR and that Net::FTP's own
\&\fBrmdir()\fR uses for \s-1MLSD.\s0 That interface has been stable for many releases.
.PP
The hashref shape is kept consistent with the \s-1SFTP\s0 and Local transports so
that consumers of \fBlist_files()\fR (including out-of-tree ones) can rely on the
same structure regardless of transport type.
.PP
\fI_perms_from_mlsd_facts\fR
.IX Subsection "_perms_from_mlsd_facts"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $perms = $self\->_perms_from_mlsd_facts( \e%fact, $type );
.Ve
.PP
Converts \s-1MLSD\s0 facts into the same \f(CW"%04o"\fR Unix permission-bits string used
by the Local and \s-1SFTP\s0 transports.
.PP
Prefers the \f(CW\*(C`UNIX.mode\*(C'\fR vendor-extension fact (exact Unix octal
permissions) when the server provides it. Otherwise approximates from the
\&\s-1RFC 3659\s0 \f(CW\*(C`perm\*(C'\fR fact, which describes only what the connected user is
allowed to do (append/create/delete/rename/list/mkdir/read/write/etc.), not
a real owner/group/other split \- the resulting triad is mirrored across
owner/group/other since that distinction isn't available. Returns undef if
neither fact is present.
.PP
\fI_mtime_from_mlsd_fact\fR
.IX Subsection "_mtime_from_mlsd_fact"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $mtime = $self\->_mtime_from_mlsd_fact( $fact\->{modify} );
.Ve
.PP
Converts an \s-1MLSD\s0 \f(CW\*(C`modify\*(C'\fR fact (\s-1RFC 3659\s0 \f(CW\*(C`YYYYMMDDHHMMSS[.sss]\*(C'\fR format,
always \s-1UTC\s0) into a Unix epoch timestamp. Returns undef if the fact is
missing or doesn't match the expected format.
.PP
\fIrename_file\fR
.IX Subsection "rename_file"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    my $success = $server\->rename_file($old_name, $new_name);
.Ve
.PP
Renames a file on the server connection.
.PP
Returns true on success or undefined on failure.
.PP
\fIdisconnect\fR
.IX Subsection "disconnect"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    $server\->disconnect();
.Ve
.PP
Disconnects from the \s-1FTP\s0 server.
.PP
\fI_abort_operation\fR
.IX Subsection "_abort_operation"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    return $self\->_abort_operation($operation);
.Ve
.PP
Records an error message for the named operation, aborts any in-progress
transfer on the current connection and returns nothing, so callers can
\&\f(CW\*(C`return $self\->_abort_operation($operation)\*(C'\fR on failure.
.PP
\fI\s-1DESTROY\s0\fR
.IX Subsection "DESTROY"
.PP
Ensure proper cleanup of \s-1FTP\s0 connections
